0️⃣ Move Zeroes to the End

The goal is to move all the "0"s in an array to the end, while maintaining the relative order of the non-zero elements.

For example:

"nums = [0, 1, 0, 3, 12]"

After moving zeroes:

"[1, 3, 12, 0, 0]"

💡 Approach — Two Pointer Technique

We can solve this problem efficiently using the two-pointer technique.

1. Maintain a pointer "j" that keeps track of the position where the next non-zero element should be placed.
2. Traverse the array using another pointer "i".
3. Whenever a non-zero element is found, swap it with the element at position "j".
4. Increment "j" after placing the non-zero element.
5. Continue until the entire array is traversed.
6. As a result, all non-zero elements come to the beginning in their original order, and all zeroes are moved to the end.

🧠 Key Pattern

Two Pointers + In-place Swapping

The array is modified directly without using an extra array.

⏱️ Complexity

Time Complexity: "O(n)" — We traverse the array only once.

Space Complexity: "O(1)" — No extra array or significant additional space is used.

##Solution

class Solution {
    public void moveZeroes(int[] nums) {
        int count=0;
        int temp=0;
        for(int i=0;i<nums.length;i++)
        {
            if(nums[i]!=0)
            {
               temp=nums[i];
               nums[i]=nums[count];
               nums[count]=temp;
               count++;
            }
        }
    }
}
